WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:07.100
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.100
Now that we know about the sum of products, let's move on to the sum of minterms.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.100
Sum of minterms form is also called canonical form somewhere.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:25.100
And minterms are actually a special form of sum of products.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:32.900
In this special form, each product needs to be in the form of so-called mean term.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.700
What does that mean?

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:46.590
That if we have three variables or three literals in our function or our expression, each of

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:51.590
those literals need to appear exactly once

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.110
in this product term.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:02.050
So if we have function that has A, B, and C,

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:10.520
that function has three literals.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.220
You can think about literals

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.920
in terms of your input variables

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.440
if that is easier for you.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:42.070
So, with respect to this function a c prime, is this a product? Yes. Is this a min term?

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:53.670
a b c here, a b c here, a c here? No, it's not min term. How about a b c prime? Is it

00:01:53.670 --> 00:02:03.800
the product? Yes. Is it a special kind of product that is called midterm? I can find

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:11.800
A, B and C. It doesn't matter if the literal is in its original form or it's

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:20.280
complemented, it needs to appear there. So it is midterm. Yes, midterm. Yes,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:29.960
especially around midterm I get to mix up min terms in midterm. How about this

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:45.070
one? And we had that. Is it a product? No. And because it's not a product then

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:57.280
it's not many term as well. Let's look at the sum of many terms. So Y is equal to

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:09.970
ABC or AB prime C. Mind you, M is lowercase M here because there is something

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:14.970
that is called a max term but right now we are not going to cover that. I think

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:25.130
it's covered in your book. So is this a sum of many terms? It's a sum of products, definitely.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:35.560
In each product it has three literals. Yes, it is. How about if we say that y is a function

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:46.840
of a and b and c. If we have another y that is a b or a b prime c, is it sum of products?

00:03:46.840 --> 00:04:11.710
Yes, is it sum of min terms? Nope, because here we are missing one. How about, well this

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:16.710
This one is not Simon product and not some of Mentherms.

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:22.170
Can it be made into some of products?

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:23.630
Yes.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.790
Can it be made into some of Mentherms?

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:27.750
Yes.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.750
So let's pause here

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.430
and go into making some of Mentherms

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.110
